Input: array A of $n$ start-finish time pairs: A[i].s, A[i].f

```text
greedy-task-scheduling(A) {
 sort A by start times so that A[1].s <= A[2].s <= ... <= A[n].s
 Create an array channel of length n; // at the end, channel[i] = index of channel for task i.
 Create list F of the final finish time of each active channel.
 for (i = 1 to n) do { // classify the ith request
  addedToSchedule = false // track if A[i] has been added to a channel
  for (j = 1 to length(F)) do {
   if [A[i].s >= F[j] {\\add A[i] to channel j.
    F[j] = A[i].f; channel[i] = j; addedToSchedule = true
    break
   } }
  if not addedToSchedule{ // create a new channel
   F.add(A[i].f); channel[i] = length(A);
  } }
 return A, channel //need to return reordered A
}
```

Runtime:

- before the loop we do $O(n)$ work to setup,
- the outerloop runs $n$ iterations, each iteration involves some constant work together with an inner loop that considers all active channels (at most $n$ iterations) and does constant work per iteration.
Thus, we have $O(n^2)$ time in total.
- Note that the implementation can be improved to $O(n \log n)$ by (similarly to 1. above) using a min-heap to track the finish times F of the active channels (keyed by these finish times). This change would replace the inner loop by checking the min-heap if the minimum value is compatible with the start time A[i].s (if so, we increase the key of this minimum element; otherwise, we add a new channel).
